speechtext:
I do not know that I did;
I probably was not well enough posted. I was not a minister as my hon. friend was, and was not familiar with the amounts. My hon. friend who in this house and throughout the country boasts that he is such a great supporter of public ownership has been one of the deadliest enemies of public ownership in this country. He sat in a government that not only permitted but, I repeat, encouraged the expenditure of hundreds of millions of dollars by the Canadian National Railways that never should have been expended. He sat as member of a government which permitted hotels to be built at Halifax, at Saskatoon and at Vancouver; that permitted railway lines to be purchased! all over the east and to some extent in the we6t; that authorized the expenditure of $50,000,000 for the Montreal terminals and of $40,000,000 to $50,000,000 for the construction of branch lines, perhaps half of which were needed, in the west. He was a member of the government which permitted ships costing $8,000,000 to be built on the Pacific coast which were never used and which are rotting at the docks to-day.
I had a right to say that the pork barrel was rolled from the Atlantic to the Pacific, I have no doubt under the direction of the government of which my hon. friend was a member. So we had the Canadian National spending these huge sums of money. There was the hotel at Halifax, which was not required at all since the Canadian National had agreed to go in with the Canadian Pacific for the construction of a hotel. That hotel was built, but a second hotel was built also, costing $2,500,000. There was a hotel built in Saskatoon costing $4,000,000, when the best that could have been expected was a million dollar hotel; that was the largest hotel that possibly could have been expected to pay its way. Then they spent $11,000,000 on the hotel in Vancouver, where no hotel was needed at all. That city is over-hoteled to-day, without that Canadian National hotel ever being opened.
